# CC3PO Insights — Development Rules

## CRITICAL: Mobile Safari / WebKit Text Visibility

Safari on iOS has a known rendering bug where `color:` declarations are ignored inside
elements with gradient backgrounds or `background-clip: text`. This causes text to appear
invisible (black on dark background) on mobile devices.

### MANDATORY Rules for ALL CSS changes:

1. **Every visible text element MUST have `-webkit-text-fill-color` alongside `color`**
   ```css
   /* ❌ WRONG — invisible on Safari iOS */
   .my-title { color: #fff; }

   /* ✅ CORRECT — works everywhere */
   .my-title { color: #ffffff; -webkit-text-fill-color: #ffffff; }
   ```

2. **NEVER use gradient text** (`background-clip: text` + `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent`)
   - This makes text invisible on ~40% of mobile devices
   - Use solid colors only: `color: #ffffff; -webkit-text-fill-color: #ffffff;`
   - If a gradient effect is needed, use a subtle text-shadow instead

3. **Always add solid `background:` on card/overlay content areas**
   ```css
   /* ❌ WRONG — gradient-only background can cause transparency bugs */
   .card-content { background: linear-gradient(145deg, #141418, #0f0f11); }

   /* ✅ CORRECT — solid fallback first */
   .card-content {
     background: #141418;
     background: linear-gradient(145deg, #141418, #0f0f11);
   }
   ```

4. **Headings inside `.prose` MUST use `!important` on color**
   - The `.prose` base rule sets `color: #d4d4d8` which overrides component-scoped h2/h3 styles
   - Always use: `.prose h2 { color: #ffffff !important; -webkit-text-fill-color: #ffffff !important; }`

5. **Before pushing, verify text visibility with browser DevTools:**
   - Open Safari Web Inspector or Chrome DevTools mobile emulation
   - Check that `getComputedStyle(el).webkitTextFillColor` returns `rgb(255, 255, 255)` for headings
   - Check that NO element has `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent`

## Image Assets

- All blog images go in `/public/blog-images/`
- Every `image:` frontmatter field in `.mdx`/`.md` files MUST have a matching file in `/public/blog-images/`
- Run `grep -rh "^image:" src/content/blog/ | while read line; do ... done` to verify none are missing
- Provide both `.png` and `.webp` versions when possible (use `cwebp -q 80 input.png -o output.webp`)

## Build & Deploy

- `npm run build` → builds to `dist/`
- Push to `master` → Netlify auto-deploys
- Netlify caches CSS by content hash — changes appear when the hash changes (usually 1-2 min after push)

## Architecture

- Astro static site, dark theme (`--bg: #0a0a0b`, `--card: #141418`, `--text: #fff`)
- Component-scoped styles via Astro `<style>` blocks (add `data-astro-cid-*` attributes)
- Global styles in `src/styles/global.css`
- Blog content in `src/content/blog/` (`.mdx` and `.md` files)
- Authors defined in `src/data/authors.ts`
## MDX Content (Critical)

Astro's `<Content />` component renders MDX/Markdown elements WITHOUT data-astro-cid
attributes. This means scoped CSS selectors like `.prose[cid] h2[cid]` will NEVER match
MDX-generated heading elements. Always use `:global()` selectors for MDX content styling:

```css
/* ❌ WRONG — scoped selector won't match MDX h2 elements */
.prose h2 { color: #fff; }

/* ✅ CORRECT — :global() compiles to unscoped rule that matches all h2 in .prose */
.prose :global(h2) { color: #fff !important; -webkit-text-fill-color: #fff !important; }
```

Always verify text visibility with `getComputedStyle(el).webkitTextFillColor` in DevTools.
